# base
systemd-sysv
linux-image-amd64
# Broad firmware (all vendors: GPU, WiFi, Bluetooth, audio DSP, NICs) --
# costs ~850M installed, but the target cabinet's actual hardware isn't
# known yet, so a narrower vendor-specific set (tried once, reverted)
# risks silently breaking WiFi/Bluetooth/audio on real hardware. Revisit
# once target hardware is confirmed (see Open Decisions).
firmware-linux

# GPU / Vulkan
mesa-vulkan-drivers
libvulkan1
vulkan-tools
libgl1-mesa-dri

# Wayland compositor. Hyprland, not weston (migrated -- see step 5 in
# notes/vpinos.md for why: weston's app-ids= output routing can't
# distinguish vpinball's/vpinfe's own separate windows from each other,
# since they don't set distinct app-ids; Hyprland's window rules can
# match by window TITLE instead, which vpinball/vpinfe's windows already
# have distinct ones of). trixie doesn't package Hyprland at all --
# comes from trixie-backports, pinned in config/archives/vpinos-backports.pref
# alongside the kernel/Mesa/AMD firmware (same reasoning: trixie's own
# version predates what's needed). libxkbcommon0 is pinned there too --
# Hyprland needs a newer one than trixie ships, pulled in automatically
# as a dependency once pinned, not listed here explicitly.
hyprland
seatd
libwayland-client0
libwayland-egl1
libdrm2
libgbm1
libinput10
libudev1
# wayland-info: compositor-agnostic output/name enumeration, works
# across any compositor via the standard wl_output + XDG output
# protocol extension.
wayland-utils
# wlr-randr: wlroots-specific output management (wlr-output-management-
# unstable-v1) -- lets output mode/position/transform be queried and
# *changed*, not just enumerated like wayland-info. Added on request;
# not independently confirmed that Hyprland's own compositor library
# (Aquamarine, broadly wlroots-compatible) implements that protocol.
wlr-randr

# python3-tk: Tk/Tcl bindings for python3's built-in tkinter -- used by
# vpinos-detect-monitors.py's monitor-identification menu (real
# clickable GUI buttons, not a terminal menu). Tk has no native Wayland
# backend in trixie's version, so it runs over Xwayland (already
# enabled in hyprland.conf) like Calamares does.
python3-tk

# input / audio
alsa-utils

# remote/debug access
openssh-server
# foot: a real Wayland client terminal for manual debugging (see
# `launch.sh shell /usr/bin/foot` in notes/vpinos.md step 5) -- weston's
# bundled weston-terminal filled this role before the Hyprland migration
# and no longer exists. In plain trixie (not backports), 662K installed,
# and nearly all its deps (libwayland-client0, libxkbcommon0, ...) are
# already pulled in by Hyprland itself, so the marginal cost is small.
foot

# networking -- confirmed directly this was missing entirely, not a
# regression from anything recent: even on the live session, vpinfe's
# own theme-registry fetch (a GitHub raw.githubusercontent.com request)
# failed to resolve DNS, and on an installed system `ip a` showed the
# ethernet interface in DOWN state with no `/etc/network/interfaces`
# stanza for it at all (`ifup` fails outright: "unknown interface").
# This image never had ANY mechanism to bring an interface up --
# NetworkManager auto-configures any interface via DHCP with zero
# per-interface config needed, unlike bare `ifupdown`, and covers WiFi
# too whenever real cabinet hardware needs it (matches the broad-
# firmware decision -- target hardware still unknown, see Open
# Decisions).
network-manager

# systemd-timesyncd: NTP clock sync. Nothing in the image set the clock
# before this -- it just trusted the machine's hardware clock. If that
# was wrong (commonly: RTC holding local time, or a dead CMOS battery),
# `apt update` failed with "Release file ... is not valid yet / Not live
# until <time>" because the signed InRelease files are dated in the
# clock's future. Installed by apt's postinst, which enables it, so it
# syncs as soon as NetworkManager brings the network up.
systemd-timesyncd

# python3: general-purpose interpreter for scripting/maintenance on the
# cabinet, in both the live session and the installed system (the
# installed system is a copy of this same filesystem). Listed explicitly
# so apt marks it manually installed and the installer's post-install
# `autoremove` can't sweep it away.
python3

# sudo: never listed here before -- it was only ever present because
# live-config transitively pulls it in (needed for live-config's own
# 0040-sudo component, which grants the live `user` account passwordless
# sudo at boot). That made apt mark it auto-installed, not manual --
# and that's a real bug, confirmed via a Calamares installer test:
# calamares's stock packages.conf purges live-config/live-boot post-
# install (correct -- those are live-only), and the follow-up
# `apt-get autoremove` correctly saw sudo as now-orphaned and tried to
# remove it too. dpkg's own prerm safety check refused ("no root
# password has been set... Refusing to remove sudo"), which halted the
# whole install with a generic "Package Manager error" in the UI.
# Listing it explicitly here marks it manual, exempting it from any
# future autoremove sweep -- same as every other package in this list.
sudo

# curl: used by 0150-install-chrome.hook.chroot to fetch Google's .deb
# (vpinball/vpinfe themselves come from the VPinOS apt repository, see
# 0100-install-vpinos-packages.hook.chroot -- their runtime deps resolve
# from the normal Debian mirrors, so no toolchain or -dev headers here)
curl

# Installer (live-then-install, like Mint/MX). calamares-settings-debian:
# Debian's own official branding/config module (used by Debian's own
# live images) -- pulls in calamares itself plus the tools its modules
# need (rsync, cryptsetup, pkexec).
#
# Runs under Xwayland (`xwayland` package; Hyprland's own built-in
# Xwayland support, `xwayland { enabled = true }`), not native Wayland --
# confirmed directly under weston that calamares's Wayland support
# is unreliable: even with QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland forced and
# qt6-wayland installed, its main window rendered fine but its cancel
# confirmation popup had readable text with zero button/panel chrome
# (tried forcing QT_QUICK_CONTROLS_STYLE=Basic -- no change), and a
# separate test run crashed outright with a raw Xlib "Cannot open
# display" error -- something inside calamares calls XOpenDisplay()
# directly regardless of QT_QPA_PLATFORM. Two independent
# community-known workarounds for calamares-under-VM issues both target
# XCB specifically, not Wayland, which lines up. See launch.sh for the
# installer-specific X11 environment (kept isolated from the Wayland
# path every other client uses).
calamares
calamares-settings-debian
xwayland

# librsvg2-bin (rsvg-convert): renders our own GRUB background PNG at
# build time from config/bootloaders/splash.svg (staged into the chroot
# at /usr/share/vpinos/splash.svg, see 0070-grub-background.hook.chroot)
# -- the live ISO's own boot splash is handled entirely separately by
# live-build's binary_bootloader_splash, which never touches the
# chroot/squashfs at all, so an installed system's own update-grub
# (run by Calamares's grubcfg module) never saw it without this. Listed
# permanently rather than installed+purged transiently within the hook
# -- avoids any risk of a repeat of the sudo/autoremove bug above over
# a few MB of disk space.
librsvg2-bin

# grub-efi-amd64: needed so /etc/default/grub already has its real,
# complete defaults (GRUB_DEFAULT/GRUB_TIMEOUT/GRUB_DISTRIBUTOR/
# GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT/...) BEFORE
# 0070-grub-background.hook.chroot appends to it. Took three attempts
# to find the actual right package -- worth recording so nobody repeats
# the same dead ends:
#   1. Assumed grub2-common ships /etc/default/grub. Added it alone --
#      didn't help, file was still missing its real defaults.
#   2. Assumed live-build's `lb binary_grub-efi` stage (a separate,
#      LATER stage than hooks/live/*.hook.chroot -- confirmed via the
#      build log's own timestamps) was temporarily installing
#      grub-efi-amd64-bin and force-purging it afterward
#      (`dpkg --force`, "this is a protected package; it should not be
#      removed"), wiping our hook's edits. Added grub-efi-amd64-bin
#      explicitly too, on the theory that listing it ourselves would
#      make live-build treat it as wanted rather than disposable.
#      Confirmed via the build log that it genuinely stopped being
#      purged this time -- but the file was STILL broken, proving this
#      theory wrong too.
#   3. Added debug output directly into the hook (`cat /etc/default/grub`
#      plus `dpkg -l` right before our own edit) and rebuilt to get
#      real evidence instead of guessing a third time. Result: the file
#      didn't exist at all at hook-run time, despite `dpkg -l` showing
#      BOTH grub2-common and grub-efi-amd64-bin as fully configured
#      (`ii`) already -- neither package actually creates this file.
#      Confirmed directly in an isolated container test: installing
#      just those two leaves the file missing; installing the
#      grub-efi-amd64 METAPACKAGE on top is what actually creates it
#      (it shares postinst/debconf logic with grub-pc for the
#      interactive "install grub now" setup, which `-bin` packages
#      don't have). That's the actual fix -- replaces grub-efi-amd64-bin
#      below (grub-efi-amd64 depends on it anyway, so nothing is lost).
grub-efi-amd64
